WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — rce-vc (90s) Narrator River

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.769
First, many people and AI agents write at once,

2
00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:07.292
and a named check admits changes or records why.

3
00:00:07.792 --> 00:00:11.556
Second, tracking name-free functions instead of text lines

4
00:00:11.556 --> 00:00:15.450
lets unrelated edits pass together without false merge wars.

5
00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:23.312
Third, developers stay on ordinary Git while checking against one hundred thousand items takes seventy microseconds.

6
00:00:23.812 --> 00:00:28.427
Branching and line-diffing worked fine when small human teams merged a few

7
00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:33.355
pull requests a day. When dozens of automated tools refactor comments, variable

8
00:00:33.355 --> 00:00:38.843
names, and helper functions every second, text lines manufacture fake conflicts nonstop.

9
00:00:39.343 --> 00:00:44.671
Our standalone Rust reference splits Rust and Python files into top-level items keyed

10
00:00:44.671 --> 00:00:51.315
by normalized structure rather than names. Non-overlapping dependency closures merge without waiting, real

11
00:00:51.315 --> 00:00:56.266
overlaps rank by pre-stated cost, and exact original bytes project back to Git.

12
00:00:56.766 --> 00:01:01.918
Across forty-nine offline tests with unsafe Rust forbidden, admitting a change into a

13
00:01:01.918 --> 00:01:08.887
one-hundred-thousand-unit repository takes sixty-nine point seven to seventy-three point six microseconds and three

14
00:01:08.887 --> 00:01:14.341
hundred seventeen to three hundred sixty-five microjoules on an Apple M5 Max chip counter.

15
00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:22.143
This standalone reference runs on ordinary Rust and Python repositories today without requiring the Reality Code Engine language,

16
00:01:22.143 --> 00:01:26.955
while keeping kernel chip energy readings strictly separate from nominal cost tables.
